# Files allowed to mention redmine_reporter, Redmineup or up_acts_as_list, with the
# reason for each. Gate: script/gates/zero_reporter.sh.
#
# This list may only SHRINK, and since 2026-08-08 that is ENFORCED rather than asked for:
# an entry whose file no longer matches anything fails the gate. Adding an entry is a
# decision to take in review, with a reason written here, not a way to make the gate green.
#
# --- THE `[permanent]` MARKER ---
#
# An entry whose reason begins `[permanent]` is exempt from `ZERO_REPORTER_MODE=strict`.
# Everything else on this list is DEBT that strict refuses, which is what makes strict a
# reachable target rather than an aspiration: before 2026-08-08 strict meant "any reference
# fails, allowlist or not", and the curator's own decision below made that unpassable for
# ever. Only the importer carries the marker, and adding it to anything else is a curator
# decision, not a way to get a gate green.
#
# THE 1.0 TARGET, decided by the curator 2026-08-05: **empty except the importer.** The
# importer ships permanently, and reading the base plugin's data by name is what it is
# for — so this list cannot reach zero, and pretending otherwise would make the gate's
# own goal unreachable. Everything above the importer section must still go.
#
# Note the negative lookahead in the gate: `redmine_reporter(?!_dashboards)`. A plain
# grep for "redmine_reporter" matches this plugin's own name several hundred times.
#
# Format: <path><TAB or spaces><reason>

# --- THE DETECTION IS GONE (curator decision #1, 2026-08-14), AND SO ARE ITS THREE ENTRIES.
#
# This section listed `reporter_presence.rb`, `lib/redmine_reporter_dashboards.rb` and
# `init.rb`. It shrank twice on its way out and the history is worth two lines, because both
# steps were sessions predicting the arithmetic and getting it wrong:
#
#   T-27 (2026-08-13) decided what the detection was FOR — "`load_patches` requires
#   REPORTER_GLUE_FILES only on a true answer" — and predicted the strict count would go
#   6 -> 3 when the glue went.
#
#   S-30 deleted the glue and it went 6 -> 5, not 6 -> 3, because `reporter_present?` had a
#   SECOND consumer nobody had enumerated: `apply_reporter_patches`, installing
#   `reporter_report_content_patch` — a performance fix on the host plugin's own
#   `report_content` action. Decision #5 then marked two comment-only files `[permanent]`,
#   which took the strict-relevant count to 3.
#
#   Decision #1 withdraws renders BY that plugin, so speeding that path up became incoherent
#   (`DECISIONS-PENDING.md` §1 says so in as many words). The patch went, the detection
#   became a memo with no reader and went too, and `ZERO_REPORTER_MODE=strict` reaches its
#   1.0 target: **0 non-`[permanent]` entries.**
#
# THE LESSON, SINCE IT COST TWO PREDICTIONS: enumerate a thing's callers from the SOURCE
# (`git grep -n reporter_present?`) before quoting a destination count. Both wrong numbers
# were written by sessions that had read the code and reasoned about it instead.
#
# What deliberately did NOT join this list on the way past: T-27's upgrade diagnostic
# (`permissions/authoring_audit.rb`, its view section and nine locale files) names the base
# plugin NOWHERE — it matches the permission NAME `manage_report_templates`, which carries no
# plugin id, and its prose says "the base plugin". Naming it would have added ten entries to
# the list it is supposed to shrink, and worse, implied the audit depends on that plugin being
# installed. It does not: the grant it looks for outlives the plugin, which is the case it
# exists for.

# --- THE WIDGET SURFACE IS GONE (T-26a, 2026-08-12). This section used to list both report
# --- widgets on two surfaces, their PDF export, the optional-widget machinery, the reporter
# --- PDF patch and its ES5 shims. All deleted: `patches/report_patch.rb`,
# --- `glue/legacy/wk_legacy_shims.rb`, `reporter_report_templates.rb`, `my/_report.erb`.
# --- Nothing in this plugin PATCHES the base plugin any more.

# --- Historical record. Comments only; no executable reference.
#
# MARKED `[permanent]` BY THE CURATOR, 2026-08-13 — decision #5 in
# `docs/plan/DECISIONS-PENDING.md`, which is the authority this file's header reserves to
# them ("adding it to anything else is a curator decision"). The options were to mark both
# permanent, to reword the comments until the name disappears, or to leave the gate red;
# the curator took the first, on the ground that the comments are worth keeping and that
# is what the marker is for.
#
# Neither file references anything: both name `up_acts_as_list` / the base plugin inside a
# COMMENT, recording what a method replaced and what a migration used to couple to. Rewording
# them would lose the searchable name of the thing that was replaced, which is the one thing
# those comments are for. Strict is a claim about COUPLING, and a historical note is not
# coupling.
lib/redmine_reporter_dashboards/positioned.rb          [permanent] comments naming the vendor gem method it replaced
db/migrate/001_create_reporter_dashboards_tables.rb    [permanent] migration comment referring to the original coupling

# --- THE IMPORTER (T-02 onward). A different category from everything above, and the
# --- one entry in this file that this gate's own "empty at 1.0" goal cannot absorb.
#
# These files name reporter because reading reporter's data is what they are FOR. That
# is the opposite of coupling: the survey reads TABLES, never reporter's classes, and
# it is designed to work on a database from which the plugin has already been removed
# (that is the migration case). Nothing here loads, patches, or depends on reporter
# code — spec/adapter/import_survey_spec.rb proves it runs against bare tables.
#
# So the gate is matching a STRING here, not a dependency. Two consequences, and the
# second is a question for the curator rather than a decision taken here:
#
#   1. Removing these mentions would make the tool worse, not purer: `rake -T` has to
#      say which plugin's data it reads, the report has to name the tables it looked
#      for, and the usage groups have to say that six accessors come from paid
#      RedmineUP plugins — an operator deciding what to drop needs that.
#   2. The header above said "At 1.0 it should be empty." With an importer that ships, it
#      cannot be — and the question of what to do about that was left to the curator.
#      **ANSWERED 2026-08-08, in T-26:** the 1.0 target is "empty except the importer", and
#      the mechanism is the `[permanent]` marker rather than an exclusion hidden in the
#      gate's search paths. These four entries carry it; nothing else does, and strict mode
#      now refuses everything else on this list.
lib/redmine_reporter_dashboards/import/survey.rb         [permanent] reads reporter's tables by name; that is the task
lib/redmine_reporter_dashboards/import/plan_report.rb    [permanent] tells the operator which plugin's data was surveyed
lib/redmine_reporter_dashboards/import/runner.rb         [permanent] T-24 copies reporter's templates; naming its tables IS the migration path
lib/tasks/reporter_dashboards.rake                       [permanent] `rake -T` must say what import:plan surveys

# --- The legacy glue (T-07) IS GONE (S-30, 2026-08-13). `glue/` is deleted entirely and
# --- this entry with it. The first attempt was reverted at 166 of 249 DB-less examples
# --- red, because the tag suite used that path as its HARNESS; the suite was rebuilt onto
# --- RenderContext first, and then the deletion was a deletion.
# --- `spec/golden/scope/scope.jsonl` survives as the record of what the six sources
# --- answered. It is the one artefact here that cannot be regenerated.
